    fillet: pole-free convex corner cap so box corners stop beading
    
    emitCap tessellates a single-sign corner as a pole-and-rings fan: every
    boundary point is slerped to one shared apex. On a convex box corner that
    apex reads as a small bead in the centre and its facets swing toward it,
    so the surrounding band shades as an uneven whorl with dark, recessed-
    looking wedges where the three arcs meet -- the "corner bead".
    
    The corner is geometrically fine (a right-angle corner's rim sits exactly
    on the size-sphere about the point equidistant from the three faces), so
    this is a tessellation choice, not a size/height error. Replace the fan,
    for genuine convex corners only, with a barycentric subdivision of the
    spherical triangle bounded by the three fillet arcs: no central pole, and
    the grid lines run parallel to the three incident round-over strips so the
    cap shades as an even continuation of them.
    
    emitCorner now records which ring points are the sector-inset corners and
    hands them to emitCapTri; it runs only when the ring is exactly three
    equal-length arcs and all edges are convex. Concave caps (pockets) and any
    other ring fall through to emitCap unchanged. The cap seats its interior on
    the rim's own mean radius, so an oblique (non-90) corner -- where the rim is
    not perfectly on the cornerBall sphere -- gets no annular step either. The
    three boundary rows reuse the exact ring vertices, so the cap still welds to
    the strips.
    
    Not yet built or bench-rendered in this environment (no toolchain here);
    verified with a standalone geometry harness that the rim lies on the sphere
    and the subdivision welds pole-free. Needs a bench run to confirm on the
    sheets and check no VALID tile regresses.
    
